<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03YN">Remark <span data-tag="03YN">8.5.1.6</span> <span class="named">(Transitivity)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing objects $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. If $Y$ is a retract of $X$ and $Z$ is a retract of $Y$, then $Z$ is a retract of $X$. To prove this, it suffices to establish the analogous result for the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ (Remark <a data-tag="03YK" href="/tag/03YK">8.5.1.2</a>), which follows immediately from Remark <a data-tag="04BZ" href="/tag/04BZ">8.5.0.1</a>. </p>
</article>
